In article <2kgapn$20i@rudder.htc.com>, joelw@htc.com (Joel Weisskopf) writes:
>Hello,
>   I am having a problem and I need some help.  I am not a VMS programmer
>( or user ) by trade but I am doing some work on a VAX and need some advice.
>I am using VMS 5.5.2 and a PC product called PATHWORKS.  Included with
>PATHWORKS is a product called CTERM that allows developement of PC programs
>that use terminal emulation.  The problem that I am having is that I am
>unable to access all special function keys on a vt320 keyboard from within
>the emulation, specifically PF1-PF4, F11-F20, and the numeric keypad.
>

This may be a simple solution, maybe not, so lets get the easilest check out
first.

Many of my users are now getting PC's with a terminal emulation package.  If
the emulation package is not set up to provide the correct terminal id specifi-
cation, then they end up with an unknown device type.  Check the CTERM setup to
insure it is set to a VT320 (if that is your choice).  I would also suggest
that you check your login.com file to see if there is a SET TERMINAL/INQUIRE"
included.  This will cause VMS to send your terminal a "what are you?" escape
sequence.  Depending on how your emulation package is set up, your terminal
type should be set accordingly.  You can check the terminal setting on the VMS
system with the SHOW TERMINAL command to verify the device type.

                                                            
