Bob,
I have had up to 9 systems set up in my lab at the same time.
I know what the sales droids say, and they have been given 
good reason to say it and been advised to say it.

In a single user mode, I have run very well with 16 MB.  WIth 
9 simultaneous users, I have run very well with 32MB.  At this
point, with only Dual Ethernet as the input path, I could prove
no benefit from going to more memory.

With FDDI, I was able to support 32 users with the 32 MB config.

Now, what I mean by support is the users noticed no delay in accessing
moving retrieving writing etc files and apps.

WIth the FDDI I was able to start to really use the system. - I actually
got the processor utilization and the I/O to show they were
in use with 16 users.  At 32 users, again with 32 MB, I was able to
project that I would be better off with more memory depending
on what the users werer doing!!
I have a setup that requires non-stop operation and so I have dual systems
on line - my goal was 32 users.....based on the performance, I can do that
quite adequately with 32MB but if I really want to do some things in a slick
and fancy way, I can go to 64MB or more...it depends on my config, the
problem being addressed, and how many users..
Hope this helps.
bob

