Subject: Large SCSI disk farms?
Message-ID: <1994Feb23.142641.14@ci.org>
From: ken@ci.org (Ken Richardson)
Date: 23 Feb 94 14:26:41 MST
Organization: Compassion International, Colorado Springs CO
Lines: 20

Has anyone discovered the secret to rock-solid SCSI reliability?  Is
reliability possible for a large SCSI disk farm?  I dunno - maybe "large" and
"SCSI" are incompatible - after all, it is SCSI, not LCSI.

Our disk farm includes a couple of dozen SCSI drives dual-ported between a
MicroVax 3100/90 and a VaxStation 4000/90.  We use SCSI-to-SCSI multiplexers to
go beyond the eight-address SCSI limit, and we use single-to-differential
converters to handle the bus-length limitation.  Overall it works pretty well;
we push millions of I/Os through the farm between problems.  But we're having
trouble making it totally solid - we crash occasionally from SCSI bus errors
(e.g. fatal controller error due to command transmission failure).

The cables seem to be involved.  Supposedly we're using a good quality shielded
cable.  Nevertheless, our SCSI bus error rate seems to be very sensitive to
cable lengths (reflections? - sometimes a longer cable works better than a
shorter one), and also to cross-talk from adjacent SCSI or power cables.
Nearby modems have caused problems too.

So - what's the secret to taking the "scuzzy" out of SCSI?  Is it possible?
Thanks in advance for any insights or hints.


