>equipped AXP machine (same memory, same # drives, CD-rom, etc), the DEC
>2000 model 300, for about $10,000 less.  
> 
>What I'm trying to find out is how do these two machines compare (3100-40
>vs. 2000-300), both processing power, and I/O.  Also, what possible
>problems could we  run into with Open VMS?  I personally think we should
>go with the AXP, since this is a new development environment, so we don't
>have to worry about porting old applications to Open VMS on the AXP
>machine.  What I don't have a clear feel for is the AXP's performance.  I
>know the 3100-90 will perform better than the 3100-40 we are currently
>testing on, but I have no idea about the 2000-300 box.  As I understand
>it, the axp machine is the bottom of the line for the alpha's, so there
>are probably tradeoffs there, where the 3100-90 is the top of it's line...
> 
>Any opinions would be appreciated.


The AXP system (DEC 2000 Model 300) should win handily on CPU
performance.  As to I/O, the following is excerpted from the
currently-being-printed Systems and Options Catalog:

                     DEC 2000                   MicroVAX 3100
                     Model 300                  Model 90

      I/O Support    6-slot EISA, 4-SCSI-2      2 SCSI

      Maxium I/O     33 MB/s                    2 x 4MB/s
      Throughput

So I/O should also be better on the AXP.

Two other considerations...
   - Memory.  In general, the faster the CPU, the more memory it is able
     to use productively. 
   - Sybase itself.  You might ask them whether they have anything else
     you should be aware of in this decision (I'm not aware of anything,
     but would want to ask).

If it were my money, I would take the AXP, and would probably apply some
of the cost difference to configuring additional memory.

Qualifier: The factual portions of the above are believed correct, but
the opinions are purely my own. 

	/john


