st3a3@rosie.uh.edu (Wright, Thomas E.) writes:

>                                                 My first duty at this job
>is to find out how much RAM this DEC 5000/200 has. 

Look at the bootup messages - they will tell you. 'cnfg 3' from the
boot prompt will also show it.

Alternatively, if the machine is up, "kvar -k -s physmem -rl /vmunix"
will tell you the number of Mb the machine has. (whats physmem? a long?
I can never remember) (oh, it outputs in hexadecimal) (need to be root
to run this)

Also, try sysinfo, from usc.edu:/pub/sysinfo. This gives lots of information
about all sorts of things, including memory.

>                                         2nd, how do I determin how much RAM
>a program will need (to run at a comfortable pace) b/4 I run it?

Umm, this is a bit harder. You really just have to guess. then you run the
processes, looking at vmstat to see how much paging is going on, and use
this to tune the amount of memory that is available for the machine, and
how much you allocate to buffers and suchlike. You could also use 'monitor'
to monitor the machine's performance while the processes are running, it
should be available somewhere on gatekeeper.dec.com:/pub/DEC or something.

Anthony
