In article <CLoMrM.Ht9@acsu.buffalo.edu>, bwmurphy@acsu.buffalo.edu (Brian W. Murphy) writes:

>I need to move files from a standalone VMS machine to an Ultrix
>workstation.  The only common device between the two machines is
>a TK50 drive (scarry, huh? actually a TK70 on Ultrix).  The
>files are not ASCII.  I can handle floating point representation
>conversions, etc.  What I need to know is how to write files
>to the TK50 from the VMS side so that I can extract just the
>binary data in stream format on the Ultrix side.  (My VMS
>source hasn't provided me with the VMS file structure yet,
>but I'm betting it is fixed length 512 byte records).

If the data is fixed length records the simplest thing to
do is to:

$ MOUNT/FOREIGN/BLOCK=#/REC=# tape-device:
$ COPY file tape-device:

Chose a block size that is a nice multiple of the record size
without being larger that 32 KB or so.  For multiple files,
make sure you don't rewind between copies.

On ULTRIX use dd(1) with an appropriate block size to read
the tape.  If you have multiple files be sure to use the
no-rewind device between commands.

% dd if=/dev/tape-device of=file bs=#

The are versions of tar for VMS.  And you can ltf on ULTRIX
to ANSI labeled tapes.  These may help in case there records
aren't fixed length records.

