  I put a response (probably in vmsnet.alpha) the other day to this
  as well.

  There appear to be several seperate LK400 related problems that
  have cropped up.  We are trying to reproduce them.

  The suggestion of a new keyboard is not unreasonable.  Recent
  reports from OSF customers seem to indicate that using older rev
  LK400's seem to fix the problem that is being seen on OSF.

  To try and explain the potential source of keyboard problems, you
  first need to understand that the LK400 is a replacement for the
  LK200 - which was designed a long time before X11 was a glint in
  MIT's eye.  It powers up with defaults that do not match what the
  X11 protocol calls for.  The X11 protocol wants to see each down
  and each up key transition.  It also want to be able to program
  autorepeat on a per key basis.  To do this, the LK200/400 is
  reprogrammed to generate up/down transitions - but in this mode,
  the keyboard does not autorepeat.  So instead, autorepeat is done
  by a software timer, and the 'click' is transmitted to the LKxxx
  from the driver.  On OpenVMS AXP, unless you are using something
  like SoftPC - the main array is *not* reprogrammed, and instead
  the main array is in down-only-autorepeat mode - the driver
  fakes a up code every time it gets a down code, and the keyboard
  itself generates the autorepeat.  On OSF/1 the entire keyboard is
  put in up/down mode.

  Two types of problems can occur:

  1) Failure to init the keyboard properly

     In this case, the keyboard mode does not match what the driver
     thinks it is in.  The most obvious bug this causes is a runaway
     DELETE key.  The driver thinks the key is in up/down mode, but
     the keyboard default is down-only-autorepeat.  So, when you
     press the key the code is sent, but nothing is sent for the
     release.  The driver thinks the key is held down, and starts
     autorepeating.  Pressing the DELETE key a second time makes the
     driver think it is seeing the UP transition.  Other bad keys are
     things like needing to press RETURN twice.  This is for the
     same reason - but the RETURN key does not autorepeat.  Once you
     find yourself in this mode, the broken keys remain broken.
     Unplugging the LK400 and plugging it back in will cause a power
     up sequence, which will cause the driver to reinit the KB.

     The usual ways a failure to init occurs is a bad interaction
     in the transition from CONSOLE mode to the Window System.  This
     means at server startup, and when the console window is toggled
     due to a ^F2.  Usually it's because a key was pressed just at
     the wrong time, and the driver got confused.  The console wants
     the keyboard in it's power up state, and the window system wants
     a different state, so it's the switch between states that causes
     the problem.

     NOTE:  This can also happen from a HALT/CONTINUE.  HALT/CONTINUE
     is NOT supported on the graphics console.  You MUST be on a
     serial line console to CONTINUE after a HALT.  The window
     system state is UNDEFINED after a CONTINUE when booted from
     the graphics head.

     A lot of work has been done to try and fix these problems, and
     they should have been in V1.5.  We are looking into this.

  2) Lost character/interrupt

     When doing a autorepeat in software, the driver depends on seeing
     the UP transition.  This is either the keycode a second time
     or the ALLUPS character (which means all up/down keys are UP).
     If the driver does not see the up transition, it will assume the
     key is held down, and start to autorepeat.  If a character is
     lost, or not delivered, or if the interrupt is dropped - then
     this would be the result.

     This type failure is sporatic.  The key is not always broken,
     like it is in the other type failure mode.  Pressing the key
     a second time should fix the problem.  It may reoccur later,
     but usually this happens only when typing fast and autorepeating.
     And on VMS, it only will happen with keys like the arrow, or the
     DELEYE keys (unless SoftPC is installed, which asks for up/down
     on all keys).

     There was a bug in DEC 3000 firmware that would show this
     problem.  The timing was such that it showed up on the 3000-300
     most frequently.  This "should" be fixed in V2.4 firmware, and
     I am following this up and verifying that it was.  However,
     this would only apply to VMS, as OSF (I think) does not use
     the firmware to read the KB data.


  In any case, we are looking at the problem both here in engineering,
  and onsite with a custimer who can reproduce it frequently.

