:
:Can anyone tell me why the following two commands to csh on an ultrix
:4.2 decstation produce radically different output:
:
:/usr/ucb/finger @ remote.computer.edu
:
:/usr/ucb/finger @remote.computer.edu
:
:The first command lists all users that have accounts on the local
:machine, the second lists currently logged in users on the remote
:machine, remote.computer.edu.  This is not what I'd expect.

An "@" argument to Ultrix's finger setup just specifies to list all
users.  You could "finger @" or telnet to port 79 (the finger port) of
your Ultrix host and type "@" and get that same list of users.
"finger @ remote.computer.COM" is the same as "finger @", since
someone probably thought it was a good idea to ignore the successive
arguments to finger.  Doesn't "finger @" on an Ultrix 4.2 box crash
the box if you have a sufficiently large passwd file?

