In article <16FEB199416420571@rosie.uh.edu>, st3a3@rosie.uh.edu (Wright, Thomas E.) writes:
>
>Hello everyone,
>   I just got a job with the PHYS. dept. here at U of H as a sys. manager.
>From now on, y'all will be hearing from me alot. My first duty at this job
>is to find out how much RAM this DEC 5000/200 has. They said that it was not
>included in the information that came with the machine. Is there a command
>(in ultrix) that I can use to find this?

You can:

1.  Use the console (>> prompt) command, cnfg, to get basic system
    configuration information.  That will tell you about the CPU
    and what is in each TURBOchannel slot.  You can do the same for
    each slot:

	>> cnfg

	>> cnfg slot-#

2.  The amount of memory is printed when the system boots.  These
    messages are written to the binary error log file, which can
    be examined with the uerf(8) command:

	# uerf -R -r 300 | page

    The -R flag prints the contents of the file in reverse chronological
    order.  The -r flag prints a selected type of message.  In this case
    record type #300 which is the boot messages.

3.  Examine the kernel variable "physmem".  Multiply by the page
    size (getpagesize(2)).  Among the ways:

	dbx(1) or kvar(8)

>2nd, how do I determin how much RAM
>a program will need (to run at a comfortable pace) b/4 I run it?

For some programs you can use the size(1) command to see the
different sizes of the program segments.  This will work for
programs that don't make extensive use of the stack or allocate
memory dynamically.  I suspect most FORTRAN programs will fit
into this group.

For programs that use a lot of stack or dynamic memory, you'd
have to examine the sources to get a clue how much it uses or
run it see.

