>Hello, Phillippe - it's been a few years !!

Indeed.  And you're no longer at ACC I see.  Denmark must be a
bit more rainy than Silicon Valley!

>There are several ways to use un-numbered links. This configuration
>is one of the common ways to set up access servers.
>
>PPP is used in several network configurations:

[ text deleted ]

>(3) Dial-in access points for standalone workstations. No routing
>    to points "behind" the remote workstation are supported.
>    Generally, in such applications there is no reachability when
>    the workstation is offline. After all, the purpose of defining an
>    access class different from (2) above, is to find ways to lower
>    system overheads and support costs.
>    In such applications, it is very common for the access server to
>    lend out IP addresses for the duration of the connection.
>    In other cases, the cluster of workstations authorized to dial in
>    may be assigned addresses (a permanent one to each station) from
>    a subnet defined to be associated with the access server.
>    This, however, creates a problem if the workstations need
>to access
>    varying access points.
>

>PAP> We can't get our box to work correctly, since each dialup
>PAP> host has its own (fixed) address on another class C network
>PAP> (for routing and security purposes).
>
>There is no point in this, unless each host is permanently
>connected to
>a LAN which is to be given internet connectivity through the
>dial-up
>link.

Sure there is!  We have access control to various resources
(filesystems, printers, external networks, etc) controlled by
access lists (example: the /etc/exports file on an NFS server)
that needs an unambiguous address to name mapping.  Since DNS
updates aren't dynamic and even if they were, they probably
wouldn't be authenticated and hence not secure.

Thus the need to know that packets coming from a.b.c.d will always
come from *my* machine and *only* my machine, and hence requests
to mount /home/philip's-dir should be accepted.

Alright, so address to name mapping isn't the most infallible
authentication scheme, but just about *everything* uses it:
X, rexec/rsh/rlogin, NFS, lpd, etc...

Portable hosts without fixed addresses is a nice idea, but...
in this case we will have to through out the current defacto
authentication and access-control scheme and come up with
something else.  Smart cards or Lord knows what.

>PAP> Lastly, about routing:  the DS700 doesn't send RIP host
>PAP> routes on the ethernet for each of it's connected hosts,
>PAP> which would seem to be the simplest.  Instead, it either
>PAP> uses proxy ARP for each attached host (on the Ethernet
>PAP> side, of course) or adds on Ethernet address for each
>PAP> attached SLIP/PPP host.
>
>Sounds reasonable to me.

It's reasonable because the DS700 implementation *forces*
hosts to appear to be on the local ethernet, and hence on the
same IP subnet, and hence ARPable.  This is just to coincidental
to be palatable to me.

Change one "given" in this scenario and nothing works any more.
Host routes are a better way to go (assuming you like RIP!)...

-Philip


