Okay, the mystery has been solved.

The missing piece of the puzzle was:

	Alpha AXP(tm) Systems Firmware Release Notes
	Order Number AA-PW8YF-TE

The CD-ROM called "Alpha AXP Systems Firmware Update V2.6" (November
1993; AG-PTMWH-BE) contains a VMS file system.  If one mounts the
CD-ROM and looks at <dev>:[000000], then one can see what files are on
there (where <dev> is the device name; "$1$DKB100" in our case).

There is no "OWNERS GUIDE" (per se) for the AXP 2000/300, but there is
a book entitled "DECpc AXP 150 and DEC 2000 Model 300 AXP Customer
Technical Information" (EK-A0636-TM.001).

The Alpha AXP 2000/300 console code v1.2 knows how to boot off the
Firmware Update CD-ROM if one adheres to the Nameless Rituals of the
Cult of the Yellow Sign by typing these commands to the OpenVMS/OSF
console monitor:

	>>> set os_type flash
	>>> b -fl 0,80 <device>

...but one can only know the name of the device from which to boot by
using the SHOW DEVICE command, which is what wasn't working for us in
the first place!  Grrrr...  You can't do ANYthing unless SHOW DEVICE
is working!

If you have a model of Alpha AXP other than the 2000/300, you need to
follow a different set of directions, and may have to fiddle with
jumpers and whatnot (boooooogus!).

It turned out that in our case, the CD-ROM does not contain a version
of software later than that which has already been installed on our
system.  So, we were facing having to live with the console code's
apparently broken behavior.

Anyway, we eventually got the SHOW DEVICE command to work.  We don't
know why it decided to start working, but it might've had something to
do with the fact that we played around with the NT firmware (which we
originally didn't know we had!) and then went back.

To do that, we typed:

	>>> set os_type nt

You have to have a VT220 console device to do this.  It won't work
with a simple VT100 (boooooogus!).  Fortunately our ancient TeleVideo
terminal does a VT220 emulation.

The NT mode is apparently much friendlier than the OpenVMS/OSF mode.
---I say "apparently" because my co-worker tried this out; I'm merely
reporting what he found in the NT firmware mode.  Anyway, he used the
NT mode to set the internal system time, and then changed the os_type
back:

	>>> set os_type osf

For some reason, once back in OSF mode, the SHOW DEVICES command
worked.  A minor miracle.  In addition, the SHOW CONFIG command
indicated a new device once, but never again after that:

	IFP	?? 00 0000

I have no idea what this spurious IFP device is (floating point?
floppy?), or what the error condition is, but since it no longer
appears on the configuration list, I guess I shouldn't worry.

And now that SHOW DEVICE works, I'm happy.  I found out that the
CD-ROM device name is DKA400, so I could boot the Firmware Update
CD-ROM to find out that we didn't have a later version of the console
code, and hence no SHOW DEVICE fix, after all.  :-)



