do miss California a lot. I am here because my parents are here, and my
wife and daughter wanted to learn Danish. I think one year in the rain
will be enough, though.

PAP> We can't get our box to work correctly, since each dialup
PAP> host has its own (fixed) address on another class C network
PAP> (for routing and security purposes).

LJP> There is no point in this, unless each host is permanently
LJP> connected to a LAN which is to be given internet connectivity
LJP> through the dial-up link.

>Sure there is!  We have access control to various resources
>(filesystems, printers, external networks, etc) controlled by
>access lists (example: the /etc/exports file on an NFS server)
>that needs an unambiguous address to name mapping.  Since DNS
>updates aren't dynamic and even if they were, they probably
>wouldn't be authenticated and hence not secure.
>
>Thus the need to know that packets coming from a.b.c.d will always
>come from *my* machine and *only* my machine, and hence requests
>to mount /home/philip's-dir should be accepted.

Using IP addresses for authentication bothers me a bit; using NAMES
obtained by address-to-name translation bothers me A LOT.
Personally, I would be very uncomfortable about allowing remote
mounts over dial-up links.

PAP> Lastly, about routing:  the DS700 doesn't send RIP host
PAP> routes on the ethernet for each of it's connected hosts,
PAP> which would seem to be the simplest.  Instead, it either
PAP> uses proxy ARP for each attached host (on the Ethernet
PAP> side, of course) or adds on Ethernet address for each
PAP> attached SLIP/PPP host.

LJP> Sounds reasonable to me.
>
>It's reasonable because the DS700 implementation *forces*
>hosts to appear to be on the local ethernet, and hence on the
>same IP subnet, and hence ARPable.  This is just to coincidental
>to be palatable to me.

The Rockwell NetHopper allows proxy ARP for all routes, not just routes
to hosts in the same subnet. So if you can get the end node to ARP for
an address not on the same subnet, you're home free.  We also set up a
host route for each conencted PPP peer. This does not always work,
though, because some routed implementations do not propagate host
routes.

The NetHopper currently addresses the authentication problem by
insisting that a given remote always comes in on a pre-authorized
port. If you are paranoid, and your calls always call into the same
server, you can set up static host routes from the file server
towards the access point and be very certain that if the remote is
being spoofed, it will not get a two-way connection.

As the use of access servers spreads, the conflict between the multiple
goals of - security - low cost - ease of use - will lead us through a few
cycles of changing connection models, and we shall see what becomes
popular.
