In article <CKKE8A.LEy@accelr8.com>, jim@accelr8.com (Jim Reiss) writes:
>Hi all...we have some software which uses a SIOCPHYSADDR ioctl to read the
>physical ethernet address of an Alpha OSF/1 system.  It appears that the
>latest DECnet for OSF/1 1.3A has started mucking with the physical address,
>and so now we're getting a DECnet address back (aa00....).  Is there any
>way to get the actual hardware ethernet address so we can work around this
>new "feature"?  Suggestions much appreciated.  Thanks.

DECnet Phase IV has always, to my knowledge, changed the "physical" ethernet 
address of a device to AA-00-04-00-xx-yy; the "xx-yy" portion is computed 
by:

  yyxx = (DECnet area) * (DECnet node)

For example, if your DECnet address is 5.15, your physical ethernet address
would be (5*1025+15=5135= hex 140f) AA-00-04-00-0f-14 (hope I got all that
right). 

The hardware ethernet address, which is the address burned into the chip, 
is unchanged.

-Dan Wing, Systems Administrator, University Hospital, Denver
