Chris Kokosa (ckokosa@delphi.com) wrote:
> I wanted full-time use of the CD-ROM on our DecServer 5100, but the
> number of files on the CD (313MB) are sucking up my available inodes.
> pstat -T results in:
>      951/1992          files
>     1040/1404          gnodes
>      101/1056          processes
>       23/  75/ 172     active/reclaimable/total texts
>      197/ 963          00k swap
>  
> When the CD device is mounted (mount -t cdfs -o noversion /dev/rz6c  /Voc)
> the gnodes are fine.  After accessing the CD via a Pathworks 4.3 file
> service the gnodes almost peg the total available.  DEC explained that
> a gnode is a RAM version of an inode.  They suggested that we increase
> our Max User from 64 to 128.  This will provide more gnodes.  My system
> admin believes that this will choke our system in other areas because
> we only have 32MB of RAM.

You might search /usr/sys tree for "param.c" and modify the line that
calculates the gnodes.  It should be in /usr/sys/MIPS/BINARY, but a 
copy my already have been put in your {machinename} directory.
These are the lines in our param.c file:

/* Change addition to 2048 for gnode number.  Original, then modified lines:
#define	NGNODE (NPROC + MAXUSERS + NTEXT + 48)
*/
#define	NGNODE (NPROC + MAXUSERS + NTEXT + 48 + 2000 )
int	ngnode = NGNODE;

mike


