In article <1994Feb2.134105.4724@vax.sbu.ac.uk>, bennete@vax.sbu.ac.uk writes:

>I'm shortly going to be installing Ultrix onto a DECstation 3100.  The
>drives I plan to install onto (a pair of RZ55s in an expansion box) have
>OSF partition information on them, not Ultrix.  Does anybody know if Ultrix
>can be installed onto OSF-labelled disks, or will this be a problem?
>
The ULTRIX installation will likely have no clue that an OSF disk
label exists and will happily ignore it.  The OSF disk label lives
in the first sector of the disk, which ULTRIX will use as the boot
block.  Other disks will also have their labels overwritten when
you create file system on them.

>If it's not possible to use OSF partition info, can I successfully edit
>the partition information on an OSF system with 'disklabel' and replace the
>text with the Ultrix label?  Can anyone point me to where I can get the Ultrix
>RZ partition format?

ULTRIX doesn't use disklabels.  It puts its partition table at the end
of the superblock.  If you want to preserve the OSF partitioning, then
make a note of it and when you have ULTRIX running use chpt(8) to change
the existing partition table.

You'll have little choice in overwriting the disklabel of the system
disk.  For non-system disks there should be an option to newfs(8) that
controls whether the boot block is written.  If you don't write the
boot block I'd expect the label to stay fairly safe.

Many RZ disks use a common partitioning scheme.  Two of the most common
are:

				  The default.
A = 32,768 sectors		A = 32,768
B = 131,072 sectors		B = 131,072
C = Entire disk			C = Entire disk
G = 400 MB 			G = C - (A + B)
H = C - (A + B + G)

The default partition table is used for unrecognized disks (RZxx).  The
first has been used for some of the most recent disks.  The D, E and F
partitions are usually spread over the larger of G or H, or the area used
by G AND H.  The kernel partition tables are in /sys/data/scsi_data.c.

When a file system is created that starts at the beginning of the disk,
the kernel partition table is copied to the partition table space in
the superblock.  If you intend to repartition a disk, just create a
file system on A and then chpt(8) to change it.

